so  it's it's good to have you i guess my  first question would be  how is the the covet season been for  santa claus  well it has been uh very very difficult  for  myself and of course for those of your  listeners out there who  know what really goes on the many other  gentlemen with white beards who work  in all of the malls and shopping centers  and venues around the country uh  all of them are in fact around the world  uh all of them  are under various restraints and  restrictions this year  as to uh how much work they can do the  type of work they can do  and how we can interact with the  families  yeah i can only imagine that uh uh with  uh because  your uh your uh your model is seeing  kids your model is malls and if malls  are going through what they're going  through you're probably affected so  how has how has this affected you uh you  know from the standpoint of seeing kids  you know and and going to typical malls  have you guys had to adjust tremendously  well i think that uh as everyone has  adjusted  in their own lives in their homes and  everything  uh fortunately the internet and uh  skype and zoom have allowed more people  to communicate  and keep the face-to-face contacts going  and that is what a lot of santa's are  moving over to  there are a lot working in the malls in  the shopping center but  often they could be working behind  plexiglass or  socially distance where maybe the family  is on one side of the tree  and six feet away and the other side is  santa  and possibly they're all wearing masks  or maybe the santas are wearing the  the clear face shields i can't i can see  that you have to get creative at a  season like this so  if if you don't mind us going back a  little bit with you is  how did you become santa because i know  you you you got an interesting story of  how you became santa i think it's a  problem we just go there first  well i always love santa claus i am the  oldest of 11 children in a  lovely irish catholic family so we  always had christmas in santa  and in 1969 i was serving in the army i  was in vietnam  and a friend of mine when we came back  into the base camp on december 23rd  to get our mail and have an early  christmas dinner  he put on some shaving cream as a beard  and sat in a folding chair and all of  the rest of us  soldiers sat down on his lap and had  pictures taken to  sent home to our moms and dads um  after about 15 minutes that shaving  cream started melting and falling away  so i grabbed that can of barbasol and  put some shaving cream on and  sat in the chair and and with santa for  you know some of the soldiers in  our unit uh quite an experience  little did i know that a year later i  would be home going to college  working in a department store and they  came to me and said  um we know that you like broadcasting  and you were in the  high school theatrical group can you be  santa for a couple days  and thus started a career of wearing the  red suit  and how long has i been now since that  couple days well it's 51 years this year  holy moly  51 years ago not always with the  real beard for many years it was a  theatrical beard  but as we say in the industry as i teach  in my santa school  the number one rule is we are there for  the children  very cool and very cool so what what  is i mean to become a santa and i've  heard that you've trained i don't know  through your school some nearly 4 000  other santa so tell tell me a little bit  about  the business of being a santa claus well  uh  about 20 22 years ago when i started  doing the  professional center work and my starting  to retire and everything  many of the santas didn't know each  other there were been some going to  a school and there were a couple places  that did some training  one of them charles w howard school goes  back to 1938  um and it was once a year up in michigan  and um i had written a couple chapters  for  a book a friend who was writing and my  chapters were kind of like on  the work that we do the business that we  do  and the requirements we have and  somebody said that was good and i added  more and  all of a sudden i had a full-fledged  book and someone said you should teach  this  so i started in 2003 my own santa school  and i traveled around the country going  to different cities to where the santas  lived  teaching them and as of uh  last year 500 santas and mrs claus  had received diplomas from my school 4  500  santas have received a diploma from your  school  yes how intense is the school well the  basic school  is two days it's 16 hours it covers  everything starting at the beginning  from a little of the history  of santa claus from saint nicholas right  through to  uh present day uh you know a little bit  about uh  the different characters the media some  of the uh  some of the things that influence the  development of the santa  from the uh his travels you know he  started in turkey  uh st nicholas was from turkey and as  his  stories traveled from that area the  mediterranean up into europe and  then eventually uh to this new  continent over here to the us and  americas  um all of the different countries  influenced  the person of santa claus with different  customs and different cultures  and much of it melded together in new  york  uh in the uh you know  part of the 19th century and uh we we  ended up with santa claus in fact uh  the term santa claus first time in print  i believe  was in 1862 in the harpers weekly  1862 was the first time the term santa  claus was used  yeah well it had been used but in print  i know in 1862  i think that the dutch in new york had  sinterklas  and when the british took over uh new  amsterdam and renamed it new york  they required everybody to speak english  and sinterklaas  became santa claus so the name was  mentioned  but in print 1862. how much has changed  for you the season uh  uh you said you 50 something years you  said you've been doing this  how much has things changed over the  years o